  "chromosome": "22",
  "evidence": "published",
  "summary": "Agouti (ASIP) decides where black pigment sits on a horse that can make it. One working copy, A, restricts black to the points - mane, tail, lower legs and ear rims - making a bay. The recessive a, an 11 base-pair deletion in exon 2 (c.191_201del) that leaves no working protein, lets black cover the whole body, making a black. It shows only on a horse with at least one E at extension: a chestnut (e/e) hides its agouti genotype and still passes it on, so two bays or two chestnuts can produce a black foal. UC Davis recognises these two alleles only. The proposed alleles At (seal brown) and A+ (wild bay) are widely used names that have not been validated as alleles; they are listed here only to mark them invalid. How dark a bay is depends on a separate, unidentified modifier just upstream of ASIP, near RALY, and on extension: that is the abstracted shade locus, not agouti.",
  "inheritance": "autosomal",
  "affects_coat": true,
  "alleles": [
    { "id": "A", "name": "Agouti / bay", "wild_type": true, "quality": "unverified" },
